{"retractions" [[17592186134214 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}6x\\,dy]dA,\n$$\n\nEvaluate the repeated integral above given that $D$ is a region in the $xz$-plane bounded by the lines $z=0,$ $x=0,$ and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=4.$"] [17592186134214 :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}6x\\,dy]dA & ={∬}_{D}6x[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=4}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6x(4-{x}^{2})\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24x-6{x}^{3}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate this double integral, we plot $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/assets/612c4fda39f7003024c322b6d1fa5c279815923905c8d023547a3be6b4a6805a.png)\n\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $z$ and then with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}24x-6{x}^{3}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}24x-6{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(24x-6{x}^{3})[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(24x-6{x}^{3})(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}96x-48{x}^{3}+6{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[48{x}^{2}-12{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(192-192+64)-0 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131727 17592186131728 17592186131729 17592186131730 17592186131731] "immutable_fields" [[17592186131726 :answer-field/id] [17592186131726 :answer-field/key] [17592186131726 :answer-field/type] [17592186131726 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131726 :answer-field/choices]]}
